Hi all - Jumping in with an {color:#36b37e}update{color} on the below now that I have had a chance to sync with the wider Privacy Team.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Dashcams*
{panel}

The reason why I am hesitant on offering discounts to drivers is as follows:

* From a privacy POV, we have taken the position that drivers are independent controllers of any footage recorded on their dashcams - this position allows us to sidestep a lot of privacy law issues that would otherwise arise, and is one we recently reiterated to the Estonian regulator. In order to be able to maintain this position, *we need to be able to demonstrate that we do not exercise any control over the use of the cameras*.
* As you have noted, providing drivers with dashcams to install in their cars could be interpreted as Bolt trying to exercise a form of control, which is why we avoid it. {color:#36b37e}Equally, we cannot make it mandatory for drivers to install dashcams.{color} Although offering discounts to drivers when purchasing dashcams is a step away from this, it still *flirts with the same issues*.

If there is a pressing need for offering discounts on dashcams, {color:#36b37e}*what we can do is opt for a* {color}{color:#36b37e}*_partnership_*{color}{color:#36b37e} *solution*{color}{color:#36b37e} (i.e., enter into partnerships with third-party providers for dashcam discounts). This would help to mitigate some of the risk. When taking this approach, please ensure that Privacy is looped in for reviewing the agreements with the partners. This should be consistent with the approach taken with Gab and Liis.{color}

